### Pattern B — Economy Measures (create on your earnings fact table)

These only work once your ACS median earnings data is loaded. The occupation names in C24010 are employment counts — **median earnings comes from ACS Table B24022**. If you have not loaded that table yet, create placeholder measures now and update the expressions later.

Replace `[your_earnings_column]` with the actual column name from your earnings table.

```
Median Earnings All Female =
CALCULATE(
    AVERAGE(fact_earnings_wi_county[median_earnings]),
    fact_earnings_wi_county[sex] = "Female",
    fact_earnings_wi_county[occupation] = "All"
)
```

Repeat for each combination below (12 measures total). The only thing that changes per measure is the `sex` and `occupation` filter values — match them to your actual column values.

| Measure Name | sex filter | occupation filter |
|---|---|---|
| `Median Earnings All Female` | "Female" | "All" |
| `Median Earnings All Male` | "Male" | "All" |
| `Median Earnings Mgmt Female` | "Female" | "Management, business, science, and arts occupations" |
| `Median Earnings Mgmt Male` | "Male" | "Management, business, science, and arts occupations" |
| `Median Earnings Service Female` | "Female" | "Service occupations" |
| `Median Earnings Service Male` | "Male" | "Service occupations" |
| `Median Earnings Sales Female` | "Female" | "Sales and office occupations" |
| `Median Earnings Sales Male` | "Male" | "Sales and office occupations" |
| `Median Earnings NRC Female` | "Female" | "Natural resources, construction, and maintenance occupations" |
| `Median Earnings NRC Male` | "Male" | "Natural resources, construction, and maintenance occupations" |
| `Median Earnings Prod Female` | "Female" | "Production, transportation, and material moving occupations" |
| `Median Earnings Prod Male` | "Male" | "Production, transportation, and material moving occupations" |

> The occupation filter strings above use the exact labels from ACS C24010. Match them to however the occupation column is stored in your earnings table.

